# Chapter 3: Edit.  The chapter is kept in several sources: the first holds
# the introduction, the edit screen, the keyboard, the modes and the menus; the
# others hold the work on blocks and sections, and the other commands.
.doc Edit
.chapter 3
.h1 Edit [edit]
.ix Edit
.ix Editor
.ix Edit screen
.ix Text area
.ix Status line
.ix Ruler
.ix Message line
.ix Key labels
Edit is the part of Strata in which the text of a document is typed, read,
changed and arranged.  It is the Editor, in its terminal version and in its
X11 version, and it is where a document is shown whenever it is opened from
the Document Manager ({n:str}).

The chapter has two parts.  The first describes the screen, the keys, the
modes and the menus.  The second describes the commands, by the thing that
each one works on: blocks and columns; pieces of the document; sections;
markers; search and replace; sort and count; macros and the library; the
options; and Quit.

The chapter in brief:
.table 18 42
Section | What it describes
{x:edit-screen} | The screen: status line, ruler, text, key labels.
{x:edit-keys} | Typing, special characters, deleting and moving.
{x:edit-modes} | Structured, unstructured, section, title page.
{x:edit-menus} | The {k:F12} menu bar, the pop-up lists and prompts.
{x:edit-blocks} | Append a column, a block or a section; join.
{x:edit-copy} | Copy, cut and delete a text, column, block or section.
{x:edit-b-split} | Split a column, a block or a section.
{x:edit-b-move} | Move a piece; promote and demote a section.
{x:edit-b-paste} | Paste the clipboard.
{x:edit-sections} | Contract, expand and Go To.
{x:edit-b-outlines} | Show the document down to a level.
{x:edit-b-marker} | Putting markers into the text.
{x:edit-b-search} | Search.
{x:edit-c-find-error} | Find Next Error, the second method of Search.
{x:edit-b-replace} | Replace.
{x:edit-b-sort} | Sort the sections or blocks.
{x:edit-b-words} | Count the words.
{x:edit-c-tools} | The macros and the library of the Tools menu.
{x:edit-b-options} | The settings of the screen and the backup file.
{x:edit-b-quit} | Leaving the Editor.
.end

.h2 The Edit Screen [edit-screen]
.ix Edit screen
.ix Screen of the editor
.ix Text width
.ix Screen size
.pic pictures/edit-a-screen.png 5.5 The edit screen of a structured document: status line, ruler and text.
The edit screen is where the text of a document is typed and revised.  It
is reached by choosing Edit / Preview / Print Document in the Document Manager
and giving the name of the
document, or by Retrieve and New in the File menu.  A file that does not
exist is created; a prompt of the Document Manager offers the choice between a
structured and an unstructured document ({n:view-make-kind}).

Every command of the Editor has a key, and nothing on the screen has to be
pointed at.  The manual names the keys as they are written on the keys of a
PC keyboard; where the terminal version cannot receive a key that the X11
version can, the text says so and names the key that works in both.

The screen is divided into rows, from the top:
.table 14 46
Row | What it holds
Status line | One row, in the colors of the status line: file, attribute,
 position, section, mode.
Expanded line | One row, only when Expanded Status Line is Yes.
Ruler | One row: the tab stops and the left indent of the column.
Text area | All the rows that remain: the document.
Key labels | Two rows with the labels of the function keys (when Display
 On-Screen Help is Yes).
.end
The message line is not a row of its own.  When the key labels are shown a
message of the Editor replaces the lower of the two label rows, in bold, for
as long as the next key has not been pressed; when the labels are switched
off, a message gets one row at the foot of the screen and the text area is
one row longer while there is no message.  The next key clears the message.

The Editor uses the whole terminal or window.  The text is laid out in at
most 80 characters per line, whatever the width of the screen, and in at
least 40: on a window of 100 columns the text area is 80 characters wide and
the rest of every row is empty; on a window of 30 columns the text is laid out
in 40 characters and the right part is cut.  A screen of 25 rows leaves 21
rows for the text with the ruler and the key labels on and 20 with the
expanded line.

.h3 The Status Line [edit-status]
.ix Status line fields
.ix Modified document, asterisk
.ix Attr
.ix Pos
.ix Sect
.ix OVR
.ix Mode, status line
.ix STRUCTURED EDIT
.ix UNSTRUCTURED EDIT
.ix SECTION EDIT
.ix TITLE PAGE EDIT
The status line is the top row of the screen.  It shows what is true of the
place where the cursor is.  From left to right:
.table 13 47
Field | Meaning
Modified flag | An asterisk before the name of the file when the document
 has been changed since it was last saved or retrieved; otherwise a blank.
File name | The name of the document without its directory.  A document
 that has no name yet shows (new).  The name is padded to 12 positions.  A
 name that does not fit in the room that the line has left (at least 12
 positions, at most 60) is shown by its end, with three periods in front, so
 that the other fields keep their place.
Attr: | The attribute of the text at the cursor: Normal, Bold, Italic,
 Under, or a mix such as Bold Italic.
Pos: | The position of the cursor, counted from 1 at the left edge of
 the current column of the current block.
Sect: | The number of the section that the cursor is in, such as 2 or
 2.1.2.  Shown in a structured document only.
OVR | Shown while Overtype is on ({n:edit-b-overtype}).
Mode | At the right edge: STRUCTURED EDIT, UNSTRUCTURED EDIT, SECTION EDIT,
 or the name of an editor of a note ({n:edit-modes}).
.end
.h4 The modified flag
The asterisk appears as soon as the document is touched: when a character
is typed or deleted, when a block or a section is added, cut, pasted or
joined, when the format of a block is changed and also when sections are
contracted or expanded (the state of the sections is saved with the
document).  It goes when the document is saved ({k:Ctrl-S}) and when another
document is retrieved or begun.  Quit prompts for what to do when the asterisk
is
shown ({n:edit-b-quit}).  Autosave (on by default, every 120 seconds; the
Setup of the Document Manager changes it) saves a changed document under its
own name when the interval has passed, and the asterisk goes with it.
.h4 Attr
The attribute is the last attribute change that stands in front of the cursor
in the column.  {k:Ctrl-B}, {k:Ctrl-U}, {k:Alt-I} and {k:Ctrl-N} put such a
change into the text ({n:edit-typing}).  The names are Normal, Bold, Italic,
Under (the underscore), Sub and Super (subscript and superscript, which a
document from elsewhere can have) and Special (any other attribute); when
several are on at once they are written one after the other, such as "Bold
Italic".  The field does not show markers: the
cursor on a marker shows the attribute of the text before it.
.h4 Pos
Pos counts the positions from the left edge of the column that the cursor is
in: a headline begins to the right of its section tag, so the cursor on the
first letter of the headline "1 Quarterly report" shows Pos: 3, and the
indent of a paragraph counts in the same way.  A new column begins again
at 1.  Pos is a position on the screen, not a count of characters: a tab
that takes the cursor from position 6 to position 11 moves Pos by five.  The
number can be as large as the width of the column.
.h4 Sect
The section number is built from the position of the section in the tree:
the first top-level section is 1, its first subsection is 1.1, and so on.
The numbers follow the tree when sections are added, moved, cut or
pasted.  An unstructured document shows no Sect field, because the whole text
is one section.  The headline of the section and its blocks all show the same
number.
.h4 The expanded line
.ix Expanded Status Line
.ix Just
.ix Indent
.ix L, left block indent
.ix Headline, status line
When Expanded Status Line is Yes in Global, Options ({n:edit-b-options}), a
second row follows the status line and shows the format that applies to the
block and column under the cursor, after all the levels of format have been
resolved:
.table 9 51
Field | Values and meaning
Just: | Left, Right, Center or Even: the justification of the column.
Indent: | None, First, All but First or All: which lines are indented.
L: | The left indent of the block, in characters.
Headline | Shown when the block under the cursor is a headline.
.end
.code
 report.doc   Attr: Normal       Pos: 3   Sect: 1              STRUCTURED EDIT
 Just: Left    Indent: None           L: 0   Headline
.end
The switch is kept with the user's other settings, so the next session of
the Editor starts in the same way.

.h3 The Ruler [edit-ruler]
.ix Ruler
.ix Tab stops on the ruler
.ix Left block indent on the ruler
.ix Tab types
The ruler is the row under the status line (or under the expanded line).  It
is drawn in dots, one for each position, and shows the format of the column
that the cursor is in: when the cursor moves to another column of the block,
the ruler moves with it so that its first position is above the left edge of
that column.  It cannot be switched off.
.table 13 47
Symbol | Meaning
dot | A position of the column, with nothing set.
small block | A position of the left indent of the block (the L: value).
right arrowhead | A left tab stop: the text after the tab begins here.
left arrowhead | A right tab stop: the text after the tab ends here.
^ | A center tab stop.
. | A decimal tab stop: the decimal point stands here.
, | A decimal tab stop that uses the comma.
# | A tab stop of the type N.
.end
The stops come from the format of the column (the tab stops of Global,
Format or of the local format, {n:format}) and are counted from the
left indent of the block.  The ruler of a headline can differ from that of a
paragraph in the same document, because they have separate formats: in the
sample document of the manual the headline has no tab stop and the text has
one at position 5.  A tab typed with {k:Tab} goes to the next stop to the
right of the cursor.

.h3 The text area [edit-text-area]
.ix Text area
.ix End-of-line character
.ix Tab character, display
.ix Block rule
.ix Section rule
.ix Column separator
.ix Highlight, display
.ix Cursor, display
.ix Dots for spaces
.pic pictures/edit-a-flat.png 5 An unstructured document with a tab and the end-of-line characters.
The text area shows the document from the line at the top down to the
bottom of the screen.  The document is a column of blocks, and a block is
made of one or more columns side by side ({n:view} explains the elements).
Everything in the area is drawn from those elements:
.table 17 43
What is drawn | How it looks
Text | In the text color; a line longer than its column wraps.
End-of-line | A left arrowhead after the last character of each line.
Tab | A right arrowhead, grey, where a tab was typed.
Block rule | A thin line across the area between two blocks.
Section rule | A double line over the headline of a section.
Headline | In bold, after the section tag (1, 2.1 ...).
Indent of a level | Three positions for every level of the section.
Column separator | A dim colon between two columns of a block.
Marker | In the form of its label, in a color of its own.
Cursor | An amber block (X11) or the cursor of the terminal.
Highlight | The area chosen for Copy, Cut, Move: the colors of the highlight.
Fold mark | A + or * in the section rule ({n:edit-contract}).
.end
.h4 The end-of-line and tab characters
The arrowheads that end a line and mark a tab are not text: they show the
places where the document holds an end of line and a tab.  They are the
default.  Global, Options has two settings for them: Display Tab &
End-of-Line Characters? (Yes by default) and Display Dots for Spaces? (No by
default), which draws a dot in place of every space ({n:edit-b-options}).
Every block ends with an end-of-line character; an empty block is one
arrowhead on a line.  Enter adds another one inside the block.
.h4 The rules between the blocks
Between two blocks the area draws a thin rule across the width of the text.
In a structured document, the rule above the first block of a section (its
headline) is double and starts three positions further to the right for each
level of the section.  The double rule is also where the + or * of a
contracted section is drawn.  An unstructured document has thin rules only.
.h4 Several columns
A block of several columns is drawn with its columns side by side, each
column laid out in its own width, with a dim colon between two columns.
The ruler and Pos follow the column that holds the cursor.
.pic pictures/edit-a-blocks.png 5 A block of two columns.  The ruler is above the second column, where the cursor is.
.h4 The cursor and the highlight
The cursor is the one position where typing goes.  On the end-of-line
character at the end of a column it is on the arrowhead.  While an area is
highlighted for Copy, Cut, Delete or Move the area is drawn in the
colors of the highlight and grows or shrinks as the cursor moves
({n:edit-highlight}).
.h4 What scrolls
The text area scrolls by lines when the cursor reaches the edge: with a
text area of nine rows or more the Editor keeps two rows between the cursor
and the edge, so the lines above and below the cursor stay in view; with
fewer rows the cursor can reach the edge.  {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} move the
cursor by half the number of rows of the area, and the area follows.

.h3 The key labels [edit-labels]
.ix Key labels
.ix Function key labels
.ix Display On-Screen Help
.ix On-screen help
.ix Alt labels
.ix Ctrl labels
The two rows at the foot of the screen show what {k:F1} to {k:F12} do, six
keys in each row.  They are on
when Display On-Screen Help is Yes in Global, Options (the default).  The
first row shows {k:F1}, {k:F3}, {k:F5}, {k:F7}, {k:F9} and {k:F11}, the second
{k:F2}, {k:F4}, {k:F6}, {k:F8}, {k:F10} and {k:F12}.  A key that has no command
has an empty label.
In the X11 version the labels change while a modifier key is held down; a
terminal does not report a modifier key by itself, so the usual labels are
always shown.  The labels of the keys that have a command are:
.table 8 8 12 16
Key | None | Ctrl | Alt
{k:F1} | Help | Learn |
{k:F2} | Split | Math | Append
{k:F3} | Copy | Lib Copy | Join
{k:F4} | Cut | Lib Cut | Delete
{k:F5} | Paste | Lib Paste | Overtype
{k:F6} | Format | Case | Set Attr
{k:F7} | Move | Prev Error | Go To
{k:F8} | Table | Next Error | Border
{k:F9} | Search | Again | Replace
{k:F10} | | Undo |
{k:F11} | | Redo |
{k:F12} | Menu | Marker | Marker
.end
When the Shift key is held down all the labels are empty.  Alt wins over Ctrl
and Ctrl over Shift when more than one is held.  {k:Alt-F1}, {k:Alt-F10} and
{k:Alt-F11} have no label and no command: pressing one gives the message
"Alt-F1: not available" (with the number of the key).
{x:edit-key-tables} lists the command behind each label.

.h2 Using the keyboard to edit [edit-keys]
.ix Keyboard
.ix Keys, editing
.ix Keys, every one
.ix Function keys
.ix Terminal keys
.ix X11 keys
.ix Ctrl-F menu
The tables below list the keys of the edit screen.  Where an entry says
"terminal" the key is received by the terminal version only when the
terminal sends it on: {c:xterm} and the terminals that follow it do, and some
others do not, and then the key acts as the key
without the modifier.  Some terminals and window managers keep keys for
themselves; the last part of the section lists them and the keys that always
work.  If a key does nothing useful in your terminal, run the Editor in the
X11 version, or use Go To ({n:edit-goto}) and the Alt commands menu
({n:edit-altmenu}).

.h3 Typing Text [edit-typing]
.ix Typing text
.ix Characters, typing
.ix Enter
.ix Tab
.ix Character set
.ix Attributes, keys
.ix Bold
.ix Italics
.ix Underscore
.ix Normal text
.ix Ctrl-B
.ix Ctrl-U
.ix Ctrl-N
.ix Alt-I
.ix Ctrl-H
.ix Hanging indent
Text is typed at the cursor.  A key that makes a character puts it into the
text, in front of the cursor, and the cursor moves past it.  The text
after it is pushed to the right (or, in Overtype mode, replaced; {n:edit-b-overtype}).  The text wraps by itself at the edge of the column; no
key is pressed at the end of a line.
.table 17 43
Key | What it does
A character | Inserted at the cursor.
{k:Enter} | Ends the line: an end-of-line is put in, in the block.
{k:Tab} | Puts in a tab; the text goes to the next tab stop.
{k:Ctrl-B} | Bold on, or off if it is on.
{k:Alt-I} | Italics on or off (see below).
{k:Ctrl-U} | Underscore on or off.
{k:Ctrl-N} | Normal text: all attributes off.
{k:Alt-F6} | The menu Set Attributes.
{k:Ctrl-H} | Hanging indent at the cursor.
{k:Alt-F12} | The Marker command ({n:edit-b-marker}).
{k:Alt-F5} | Overtype on or off.
.end
.h4 Enter
{k:Enter} puts an end-of-line character into the text and starts a new line
in the same block.  It does not start a new block, a new section or a new
paragraph in the sense of the format: the block is one unit for Copy, Cut,
Join and the formats, and the lines that Enter makes belong to it.  A new
block is made with {k:Ctrl-A} or {k:Ctrl-O}, a block is split in two with
{k:Ctrl-V} or {k:F2}, and a new section with {k:Ins} ({n:edit-append}).  In a
headline {k:Enter} makes a second line in the headline block.
.h4 Tab
{k:Tab} inserts a tab character, drawn as an arrowhead, and the text that
follows moves to the next tab stop of the ruler to the right of the
position of the tab.  The width of the tab follows the ruler whenever the
text is laid out again.
.h4 Ctrl-Shift-Space
{k:Ctrl-Shift-Space} types a nonbreaking space ({n:edit-nbsp}).
.h4 Alt-_
{k:Alt-_} (Alt with the hyphen key and Shift) types a soft hyphen ({n:edit-shy}).
.h4 Attributes
The attribute keys do not mark a piece of text; they put an attribute
change into the text at the cursor, and everything after it, up to the next
change, has the attribute.  Type {k:Ctrl-B}, then the words, then
{k:Ctrl-B} again to have a bold phrase; or mark the end with {k:Ctrl-N}.
The change takes no room on the screen: {k:Del} and {k:Backspace} can
remove it, and Copy and Cut carry it with the text.  {k:Ctrl-I} is the
Tab key in both versions, so {k:Alt-I} is the key for italics.  A macro that is defined on an Alt-letter takes that key
first ({n:edit-c-macro-learn}), so a macro on {k:Alt-I} replaces italics.
The menu Set Attributes ({k:Alt-F6}, and Set Attribute in the Alt commands
menu) has the same four choices:
.table 16 44
Choice | Effect
Normal | The same as {k:Ctrl-N}.
Bold | The same as {k:Ctrl-B}.
Italics | The same as {k:Alt-I}.
Underscore Words | The same as {k:Ctrl-U}.
.end
.h4 Hanging indent
{k:Ctrl-H} sets a hanging indent for the block: the cursor is placed after
the characters that are to hang (a bullet and a space, a number and a
period), and {k:Ctrl-H} makes the left edge of the following lines line up
with the text after them.  The block gets a tab stop at the position of the
cursor and the indent type All but First.  With the cursor at the start of
the line the message is "Type the hanging characters first, then press
{k:Ctrl-H}" and nothing changes; on success the message is "Hanging indent set
at N".  In a terminal that sends {k:Ctrl-H} for the Backspace key, set the
terminal to send the Delete code (127) for Backspace, which is what the Editor
requires.

.h4 Text indent
.ix Text indent
.ix Ctrl-G
{k:Ctrl-G} puts the text indent character at the cursor.  The character shows
as a dim arrow.  The lines that continue the paragraph begin below the place
after the character, until the end of the paragraph; the print does the same.
Type a number or a bullet, then {k:Ctrl-G}, then the text: the text then hangs
under the first word.  Delete takes the character out like any other
character.

.h3 Typing special characters [edit-special-chars]
.ix Special characters
.ix Accented letters
.ix Character set, PC
.ix Code page 437
.ix Copyright sign
.ix Dash, typing
.ix Dead keys
The characters of a document are those of the PC character set, code page
437 ({n:appchar} lists them).  A special character is typed like any other
letter: with the key that the keyboard layout gives it.
.table 25 35
Where the character comes from | Result
A key of the keyboard layout | The character, if the set has it.
A dead key or a compose key of the system | In the terminal version the
 same, once the system has put together the letter and sent it.  The X11
 version cannot use dead keys and compose sequences; a key of the layout
 that makes the letter does.
A letter pasted in the terminal | The same as typing it.
.end
A character that the set does not hold is not put into the text.  The accented
letters of Western Europe, the German sharp s, the Spanish
inverted marks, the fractions one half and one quarter, the plus-minus sign,
the degree sign and the micro sign are in the set, so both versions can type
them.  The Greek letters of the set (the small letters alpha, delta,
epsilon, pi, sigma, tau and phi, and the capitals Gamma, Theta, Sigma, Phi and
Omega; the micro sign serves as the small mu), the infinity sign and the
characters for boxes are in the set but the X11 version cannot type them from
the keyboard; the terminal version takes them when they are pasted.  The
other Greek letters, such as beta, small gamma, small theta and small omega,
are not in the set and are lost.  The euro sign, the copyright and
registered signs, the trademark sign and the typographic dashes and quotes
are outside the set.  A symbol that the printed document
needs and that the set lacks can be had with the Equation marker, which sets
text in a mathematical style ({n:equations}), or with a Picture.

A text that is pasted into the terminal version can lose the characters that
the set does not have: look at the result when a text with special letters is
pasted.

.h3 Typing a Nonbreaking Space [edit-nbsp]
.ix Nonbreaking space
.ix Space, nonbreaking
.ix Ctrl-Shift-Space
.ix Words that stay on one line
A nonbreaking space is a space that a line is never broken at.  Use it between
the words that must stay on the same line: a title and a name ("Mr. Smith"), a
number and its unit ("10 km"), the parts of a date, or a short word and the
word that follows it.  Where an ordinary space lets the line end, the
nonbreaking space moves both words to the next line.
.table 23 37
Point | Behavior
Typing | Press {k:Ctrl-Shift-Space}.  The character is put in at the cursor
 like any letter and, in overtype mode, replaces the character under the
 cursor.  In a text field of a panel (for example the strings of Search and
 Replace) the key types the same character.  In the X11 version {k:Ctrl-Space}
 without {k:Shift} does nothing.  Most terminals send the
 same code for {k:Ctrl-Shift-Space} and {k:Ctrl-Space} (and {k:Ctrl-@}); in the terminal
 version all of them type the nonbreaking space.
Keys of a terminal | A terminal that is set to report modified keys (the
 setting is the terminal's, and its manual says how) sends
 {k:Ctrl-Shift-Space} itself, and Strata takes it.  Without that setting
 the three keys send the same code, and the result is the
 same.
On the screen | A small cap, like a bridge between the two words, so that it
 can be told from a space.  It is moved over by the cursor keys as one
 character.  The cap is only a mark on the screen: the print shows a space
 that a line is never broken at.
In a line | The line is not broken at it, on the screen and in the print.
Pasting and Import | A no-break space that is pasted in the terminal, typed by
 a keyboard layout that has the key for it, or read by Import (UTF-8 and
 Latin-1 files have it) is the same character.
Search and Replace | A search string that holds a nonbreaking space (typed with
 {k:Ctrl-Shift-Space}) matches only a nonbreaking space; a string with a space
 does not match one ({n:edit-b-replace}).  A replacement may hold it.
Spell check and Thesaurus | It ends a word, like a space.
Words and the count of words | It ends a word.
Export | A nonbreaking space stays a nonbreaking space in a UTF-8 file, a
 Latin-1 file and a file of the PC character set, and becomes a space in a 7
 bit file ({n:file-export}).
Macros | A macro keeps the key and plays it back.
.end
.h3 Typing a Soft Hyphen [edit-shy]
.ix Soft hyphen
.ix Hyphen, soft
.ix Alt-_
.ix Words, where they may be broken
A soft hyphen marks a place where a word may be broken at the end of a line.
Where TeX breaks a word in the wrong place, or does not know how to break it, type a
soft hyphen at each place where it may be broken: pro-ject, or a name
or a technical word.  The print breaks the word at a soft hyphen only when it is
at the end of a line, and then prints a hyphen; elsewhere the word is whole and
the mark is not seen.  A word with a soft hyphen is broken only where it has
one.  For a word that comes again and again, the hyphenation list
({n:str-spell-words-hyphen}) says it once for the whole print.
.table 23 37
Point | Behavior
Typing | Press {k:Alt-_}, that is {k:Alt} with {k:Shift} and the hyphen key.  The
 character is put in at the cursor like any letter and, in overtype mode,
 replaces the character under the cursor.  A terminal sends the key as
 {k:Esc} and the underscore, which needs the setting that makes {k:Alt} send
 {k:Esc} ({n:appterm-esc}).  Where the terminal or the window manager does
 not pass the key, press {k:Ctrl-F} and choose Soft hyphen (the last
 entry, {n:view-ctrlf}); it works in every terminal.  {k:Alt--} without
 {k:Shift} still contracts a section ({n:edit-contract}), and {k:Ctrl-/}
 is Undo: neither types a soft hyphen.
On the screen | A small triangle that points down, dim, between the two parts of
 the word.  It takes one cell and the cursor moves over it as over one
 character.  The screen never breaks a line at it: the mark shows where the
 print may do it.  The triangle is only a mark on the screen.
In the print | The word may be broken at it, and only at the soft hyphens that
 the word has.  If the line does not end there, nothing is printed.
Search and Replace | A search string goes through a soft hyphen of the text:
 "hyphenation" finds "hy-phen-ation" with soft hyphens, and a replacement
 takes the soft hyphens that were inside what was found.  Find at Soft Hyphen
 finds them one by one, and Replace with Find at Soft Hyphen and an empty
 replacement takes them all out ({n:edit-b-replace}).
Spell check | The word is checked without its soft hyphens, so that a word that
 is spelled right is not asked about because it is marked.
Words and the count of words | A soft hyphen does not end a word and is not
 counted as a character.
Export | A soft hyphen is not written: a text file, the screen image and the
 export for data have the word whole.
Files | A soft hyphen is the character 1F in the text of a block,
 as in other word processors of its kind; a document that has them can
 be read and written again as it is.
Macros | A macro keeps the key and plays it back.
.end
.h3 Deleting Text [edit-deleting]
.ix Deleting text
.ix Del
.ix Backspace
.ix Ctrl-D
.ix Alt-Backspace
.ix Delete a character
.ix Delete a word
.ix Delete a block
.ix Cannot delete the only block
.ix Cannot delete the only section
.ix Nothing to join here
These keys remove text without a highlight.  What is removed with a
highlight is in {x:edit-copy}.
.table 15 45
Key | Removes
{k:Del} | The character at the cursor.
{k:Backspace} | The character before the cursor.
{k:Ctrl-D} | The rest of the word and the spaces after it.
{k:Alt-Backspace} | The word before the cursor.
{k:Ctrl-K} | The block that holds the cursor (to the clipboard).
{k:Ctrl-L} | The section that holds the cursor (to the clipboard).
{k:Ctrl-X} | A piece of text, after highlighting (to the clipboard).
{k:Alt-F4} | Text, column, block or section, after highlighting.
.end
.h4 Del
{k:Del} removes the character at the cursor: a letter, a tab, an attribute
change, a marker, or the end of a line inside the block (which joins the
two lines).  At the end-of-line character that ends the last line of a
column the key has a second meaning, which depends on what the block is:
.table 25 35
The cursor is at the end of | {k:Del} does
An empty block of one column | Removes the block.
A block of one column that has text | Joins the next block to it.
The last column of a block of several | Joins the next block to it.
A column that is not the last | Nothing.
.end
The join is the one of Join, Block ({n:edit-join}): the text of the next
block follows the text of this one, the cursor stands at the place where they
meet, and the next block disappears with its format.  When there is no next
block in the section the message is "Nothing to join here".  When the empty
block is the only block of its section the whole section is removed if it is
possible; otherwise the message is "Cannot delete the only block" (in an
unstructured document, or when the section has subsections) or "Cannot delete
the only section" (when it is the only section of the document).  After the
removal the cursor goes to the end of the section before it.
.h4 Backspace
{k:Backspace} removes the character before the cursor, whatever it is, and
the cursor moves back.  At the start of the first column of a block of one
column, whose previous item is a block of one column, the block is joined to
the one before it (the text follows the text of the block before, and the
cursor is at the junction).  The first paragraph after a
headline is joined to the headline in the same way.  At the start of a headline, at the start
of a column that is not the first, or when one of the two blocks has more than
one column, nothing happens.
.h4 Ctrl-D and Alt-Backspace
{k:Ctrl-D} removes the word at the cursor from the cursor on, and the
spaces and punctuation that follow it, up to the start of the next word.
It does not take the end of the line.  On spaces, it removes the spaces up
to the next word.  At an end of line that is not the last of the column it
removes the end of line, joining the two lines.  {k:Alt-Backspace} removes
backward: the spaces and punctuation before the cursor, then the word
before them.  The cursor ends at the start of what was removed.
.note For deleting, a word is a run of plain letters and digits.  For moving
with {k:Ctrl-Left} and {k:Ctrl-Right} a word also contains accented letters
and the other characters of the upper half of the set; so {k:Ctrl-D} stops at
an accented letter where {k:Ctrl-Right} does not.

.h3 Moving around the document [edit-moving]
.ix Moving around
.ix Cursor movement
.ix Cursor keys
.ix Arrow keys
.ix Home
.ix End
.ix PgUp
.ix PgDn
.ix Ctrl-Left
.ix Ctrl-Right
.ix Ctrl-Home
.ix Ctrl-End
.ix Shift-Home
.ix Shift-End
.ix Shift-Up
.ix Shift-Down
.ix Shift-PgUp
.ix Shift-PgDn
.ix Shift-Left
.ix Shift-Right
.ix Word left
.ix Word right
.ix Beginning of document
.ix End of document
.ix Section keys
The cursor is moved with the keys of the table.  The cursor is always on a
block that can be seen: text hidden by Contract or by an outline, and text
outside the section in Section edit, is passed over.
.table 15 45
Key | The cursor goes to
{k:Left} | The previous character; across columns and blocks.
{k:Right} | The next character; across columns and blocks.
{k:Up} | The line above, in the same screen column.
{k:Down} | The line below; on the last line, its end.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | Half a screen up, half a screen down.
{k:Home} | The start of the screen line.
{k:End} | The end of the screen line.
{k:Ctrl-Left} | The start of the word before.
{k:Ctrl-Right} | The start of the next word.
{k:Ctrl-Home} | The start of the column.
{k:Ctrl-End} | The end of the column.
{k:Shift-Home} | The start of the document.
{k:Shift-End} | The end of the document.
{k:Shift-Up} | The headline of the section.
{k:Shift-Down} | The headline of the next section.
{k:Shift-PgUp} | The previous section of the same level.
{k:Shift-PgDn} | The next section of the same level.
{k:Shift-Left} | The previous column, then the parent section.
{k:Shift-Right} | The next column, then the first subsection.
{k:Alt-F7} | Go To: a section, the start or the end ({n:edit-goto}).
{k:Alt-F8} | Border: lines around columns ({n:edit-c-border}).
.end
.h4 Left and Right
{k:Left} moves back one character.  At the start of a column it goes to the
end of the column before it in the same block; at the start of the first
column it goes to the end of the last column of the block above.  {k:Right}
is the same forward: at the end of a column it goes to the next column, and
at the end of the last column to the first character of the next block.  At
the start of the document and at its end the keys stay where they are.  An
attribute change takes no room but is a character of the text: the cursor
stops once on it, without moving on the screen.  A marker is one position of
the text: the cursor stops on it.
.h4 Up and Down
{k:Up} and {k:Down} move one line of the screen.  The cursor keeps its
screen column through a run of {k:Up} and {k:Down} keys, even when a short
line lies in between, and returns to it on a long line; any other movement
sets the column again.  In a block of several columns the cursor stays in
its column while that column has lines.  At the first or the last line of a
column it goes to the block above or below, into the column of that block
that is nearest to the screen column.  {k:Down} on the last line of the
document goes to the end of that line.  A displayed equation or picture is one
line of the text ({n:edit-cursor-picture}).
.h4 Home and End
{k:Home} goes to the first character of the screen line and {k:End} to the
last character of it.  A paragraph that wraps over four screen lines has four
starts and four ends.  On the last line of a column, {k:End} stops on the
end-of-line character.
.h4 PgUp and PgDn
{k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn} move up or down by half the height of the text area
(a text area of three rows or fewer moves one row).  The keys move the
cursor, and the text scrolls to follow it.  They stop at the first and the
last line of the document.
.h4 Words
{k:Ctrl-Left} goes to the start of the word before the cursor (or to the
start of the current word when the cursor is in the middle of it), and
{k:Ctrl-Right} to the start of the next word.  A word is a run of letters,
digits and characters of the upper half of the set.  At the start of a
column {k:Ctrl-Left} acts as {k:Left}, and at the end {k:Ctrl-Right} as
{k:Right}, so a run of the key goes through the whole document.
.h4 Start and end of a column, of the document
{k:Ctrl-Home} and {k:Ctrl-End} go to the first character and to the end of
the column that holds the cursor.  {k:Shift-Home} and {k:Shift-End} go to the
first character of the first block and to the end of the last block that can
be seen.
.h4 The section keys
There is no key for the previous or the next page: the Printer makes the pages,
and the Editor does not.  The keys with {k:Shift} move over the sections of a structured document;
in an unstructured one they do nothing, except {k:Shift-Home} and
{k:Shift-End}.  They pass over the sections that are hidden by an outline or
by Section edit.
.table 14 46
Key | Rule
{k:Shift-Up} | The headline of the current section.  On the headline, the
 headline of the section before it in the text (any level).
{k:Shift-Down} | The headline of the next section in the text (any level).
 Stays at the last one.
{k:Shift-PgUp} | The previous section of the same level that has the same
 parent; stops at a section of a lower level.
{k:Shift-PgDn} | The next such section.
{k:Shift-Left} | In a block of several columns: the previous column.
 Otherwise: the headline of the parent section.
{k:Shift-Right} | In a block of several columns: the next column.
 Otherwise: the first subsection, when there is one.
.end
.h4 The cursor and a picture [edit-cursor-picture]
.ix Picture, moving the cursor over it
.ix Cursor, over a picture
A picture is one line of the text and one stop of the cursor, however many
rows of the window it covers.  {k:Down} from the line above puts the cursor on
the picture, where it stands on the first row of the image, and the next
{k:Down} puts it on the line below; {k:Up} does the same the other way.  The
end of line mark under the image is not a stop of {k:Up} and {k:Down}
({k:Right} and {k:End} reach it).  The terminal shows a picture as one row with
its name in brackets, so there is nothing more to it there.

In the X11 window the text has to move by about the height of the image when
the cursor goes onto a picture or off one.  The window does not leap by the
whole height: it moves in steps of a few rows, 0.01 seconds apart, about
0.1 seconds in all, and a key that you type meanwhile ends the steps
at once and is handled.  The top edge of a picture that comes up from the
bottom of the window shows first, and the window then slides as the cursor
goes on past it.  A picture that is taller than the text area cannot be seen
at once: while the cursor is on it {k:PgDn} and {k:Down} scroll down through
its rows a window at a time, {k:PgUp} and {k:Up} back up, and only when its
last or first row has been shown does the key leave the picture.

