.table 20 40
Key | What it does
{k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9}, {k:Alt-0} | Outline to a level, {x:edit-b-outlines}.
{k:Alt--}, {k:Alt-+}, {k:Alt-*} | Contract, expand, outline a section.
{k:Alt-[}, {k:Alt-]} | Promote, demote a section ({n:edit-b-move}).
{k:Alt-<}, {k:Alt->}, {k:Alt-,}, {k:Alt-.} | The same, for terminals.
{k:Alt-/} | Redo ({n:edit-b-undo}).
{k:Alt-I} | Italics.
{k:Alt-O} | Overtype on or off.
{k:Alt-Backspace} | Delete the word before the cursor.
Alt and a letter | A macro that has that letter (Tools, Macros).
.end
.h4 The keys of the cursor and of sections
{k:Ins} appends a section, {k:Shift-Ins} a subsection ({n:edit-append}); the
{k:Ctrl-V} and {k:Ctrl-Y} keys split ({n:edit-b-split}); the movement keys
were listed in {x:edit-moving}.

.h3 Keys that the terminal or the window manager keeps [edit-reserved]
.ix Alt-F12, taken by the window manager
.ix Shift-Ins, paste of the terminal
.ix Ctrl-F, Alt menu
.ix Terminal, keys not received
.ix Window manager keys
Some of the keys of the edit screen are used by the window manager or the
terminal itself, and the Editor never receives them.  For each, Strata has
another way.
.table 19 13 28
Key | Taken by | What to use
{k:Alt-F12} | Many desktops | {k:Ctrl-F12}, {k:Ctrl-F}
{k:Alt-F4} | Window managers | {k:Ctrl-F}, Delete
{k:Shift-Ins} | Terminals (paste) | {k:Ctrl-F}, Sub-section
{k:Alt-[} | Terminal: key prefix | {k:Alt-<}
{k:Ctrl-^} | Some keyboards | Edit menu, Section
{k:Ctrl-I} | Tab, in both versions | {k:Alt-I} for italics
{k:Ctrl-Backspace} | Terminal ({k:Ctrl-H}) | {k:Alt-Backspace}
{k:Keypad +} | Terminals | {k:Alt-+}, {k:Alt-=}
.end
.h4 The keypad
In the X11 version the three keys of the numeric keypad with a plus, a minus
and an asterisk are the keys of the outline: they work as {k:Alt-+},
{k:Alt--} and {k:Alt-*}.  A terminal sends them as the plain characters,
which would be typed into the text, so there the Alt keys are used.  In the
X11 version {k:Keypad Enter} is {k:Enter}.  With Num Lock off the keys
{k:Keypad Ins}, {k:Keypad Del}, {k:Keypad Home}, {k:Keypad End},
{k:Keypad PgUp}, {k:Keypad PgDn}, {k:Keypad Up}, {k:Keypad Down},
{k:Keypad Left} and {k:Keypad Right} are the keys that they are named after,
with the modifiers that are held; with Num Lock on {k:Keypad 0} to
{k:Keypad 9} and {k:Keypad .} type the digit or the point ({n:appx11}).  A
terminal does not send the Alt key as a modifier; it sends {k:Esc} and then
the key.  Press {k:Esc} alone, or twice, to close a list ({n:appterm-esc}).
