.h2 Modes of the Editor [edit-modes]
.ix Modes
.ix Structured edit
.ix Unstructured edit
.ix Section edit
.ix Title edit
.ix Note edit
.ix Equation edit
.ix Editors of notes
.ix Press Ins to Save Changes or Esc to Restore Previous Version
.ix Library Contents Edit
The word at the right end of the status line shows in which mode the Editor
is.  The mode changes which keys have an effect and what the Edit menu
offers.
.table 20 40
Mode | When
STRUCTURED EDIT | A document with sections.
UNSTRUCTURED EDIT | A document without sections.
SECTION EDIT | One section of a structured document.
TITLE PAGE EDIT | The title page, in a window of its own.
NOTE EDIT, and others | A note, comment, equation or library entry.
.end

.h3 Structured and Unstructured Edit [edit-b-structured]
.ix STRUCTURED command
.ix UNSTRUCTURED command
.ix Structured mode
.ix Unstructured mode
.ix Enter structured mode
.ix Enter unstructured mode
.ix Unstructured is not offered for a document of several sections
.ix Change mode of a document
A structured document is a tree of sections.  Every section has a
headline, which is its first block, then more blocks of text, and then its
subsections.  The sections are numbered from their place in the tree, and
the status line shows the number.  All the keys of this chapter work.  A new
document, made with File, New, is structured with one empty section unless
the template that you name is of an unstructured document
({n:view-make-kind}).

An unstructured document is blocks in a row, with no sections, no
headlines and no numbers: a letter, a memo, a list.  The status line has no
Sect field, the rules between the blocks are thin, and the keys of
sections do nothing, or the nearest thing: {k:Ins} and {k:Shift-Ins} append
a block of the global format (the same as {k:Ctrl-O}), {k:Ctrl-L} does
nothing, {k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9} and the keys that contract and expand give the
message "Collapse/expand works in structured documents", Go To has the section
number 1 only and the lists of Copy, Cut, Move and Delete have no Section
choice.

One kind is turned into the other from the Edit menu ({k:F12}, Edit); the entry
depends on the document and there is no key:
.table 13 47
Entry | Hint and when it is offered
Structured | "Enter structured mode".  Offered in an unstructured document.
 The letter {k:S} chooses it.
Unstructured | "Enter unstructured mode".  Offered in a structured document
 that has just one section with no subsections.  The letter {k:U} chooses it.
.end
Structured makes the whole text of the document one section.  The first block
of the text is the headline of that section, so that the first line of the
text is now numbered 1 and is shown as a headline.  Nothing else is changed:
all the other blocks stay as they are, after the headline, in the one section.
The status line shows "Sect: 1" and STRUCTURED EDIT, the cursor goes to the
start of the document, and the document is marked as changed.  After this the
commands of sections (Append section, the level keys, Split, Join,
outlines and the others) work.

Unstructured turns a structured document that has one section and no
subsections into a plain document: the headline stays as the first block (and
is no longer a headline), and the numbers go.  The cursor goes to the start.
The title page is not touched.  The commands that need sections give a
message and change nothing: {k:Ctrl-^} shows "Only a structured document has
sections", and Autosort, Section shows "Sections exist only in structured
documents".  The Edit menu has no Section entry.  Unstructured
never throws text away.
.caution To make a document of many sections unstructured, make it one
section with no subsections first (Join, Section, or Cut and Paste).  Then
the entry Unstructured is offered.  Until then the entry is not in the Edit
menu, the letter {k:U} does nothing and there is no message.

.h3 Section Edit [edit-b-section]
.ix SECTION EDIT
.ix DOCUMENT edit
.ix Edit current section only
.ix Edit entire document
.ix Section Edit mode
.ix Ctrl-^
.ix SECTION EDIT status
.ix Only a structured document has sections
.ix There is no current section
Section lifts the section that the cursor is in out of the document, with
all its subsections, so that only that part is shown and edited.  The rest of
the text is out of sight, and it cannot be reached with the cursor keys until
the mode is left with Document, the same entry under its other name.  The
two commands are one entry on the Edit menu that changes its name, and one
key, {k:Ctrl-^}.  They exist only in a structured document.
.table 21 39
Way | What it does
{k:F12}, Edit, Section | Enters Section Edit mode.  The hint line shows "Edit
 current section only [Ctrl-^]".  The letter {k:S} chooses it.
{k:F12}, Edit, Document | Leaves the mode.  The hint line shows "Edit entire
 document [Ctrl-^]".  The letter {k:D} chooses it.
{k:Ctrl-^} | Does the one that applies: enters the mode when it is off and
 leaves it when it is on.
.end
{k:Ctrl-^} is typed as {k:Ctrl-Shift-6} on most keyboards ({k:Ctrl-6} on some);
on a keyboard that does not pass it, use the menu.  {k:Esc} does not leave the
mode.
.pic pictures/edit-b-section-edit.png 5 Section Edit mode on the section 2.1.
The status line shows SECTION EDIT in place of STRUCTURED EDIT, and the
section number of the section being edited is on it as always (Sect: 2.1).  The
text area shows the headline of the section, its blocks and all its
subsections, indented as in the whole document, with their numbers unchanged:
a section numbered 2.1 is still 2.1, not 1.
.table 14 46
Point | Behavior
Which section | The one that the cursor is in at the time of the command.
 When the cursor is in a block of the body of a section, it is that section;
 when it is in a headline, it is the section of that headline.  A subsection
 that is chosen has its own subsections, but not its parent or its siblings.
The cursor | Stays where it is; it is always inside the visible part.
Cursor keys | The movement keys stop at the edges of the visible section.
 The section keys work inside it.
Editing | The editing commands work on the visible text: typing, Delete,
 Copy, Cut, Paste, Move, Split, Join, Append, and Sort of the subsections.
Replace, Count | They work on the whole document, not only on the shown
 section.
Save | The whole document is saved, not only the section shown.
Outlines | The keys that contract and expand work on the sections that are
 shown.
Edit menu | The entry reads Document while the mode is on.  Title is there
 as usual; Unstructured is offered too when the whole document is one
 section.
.end
.table 28 30
Message | When
Only a structured document has sections | {k:Ctrl-^} in an unstructured
 document.
There is no current section | The command in a document that has no section
 at the cursor: only the text before the first headline, or an empty
 document.
.end
Leaving the mode shows everything again, with the cursor where it was, and
changes nothing in the document: the mode is a view, it is not saved with the
document and does not mark it as changed.  Section Edit is used to work on a
part of a long document without the rest getting in the way: to move around a
chapter with the section keys, to sort its subsections ({n:edit-b-sort}), or
to keep from deleting something else by mistake.  Because the numbers are
those of the whole document, a cross reference in the part keeps its number.

.h3 Title Page Edit [edit-b-title]
.ix TITLE command
.ix Title page
.ix TITLE PAGE EDIT
.ix Edit title page
.ix Press Ins to Save Changes
.ix Restore Previous Version
.ix Cover page
.ix Print Title Page
.pic pictures/edit-a-title.png 5 Title Page Edit: the title page is an editor of its own.
Edit, Title ({k:F12}, Edit, Title; the letter {k:T}; the hint is "Edit title
page") opens the title page of the document in an editor of its own, so that
the page can be typed and changed without being part of the text.  The title
page is the first page of a printed document: a block or several blocks, with
the same tools as the text (formats, attributes, markers, columns).  It is
the first entry of the Edit menu in every kind of document.

The status line shows TITLE PAGE EDIT on the right, and the name of the
document is replaced by (new) when the document had no title page; the
message line shows "Press Ins to Save Changes or Esc to Restore Previous
Version".  The page is a small document of its own: it has no sections and
the commands that need them do not work.  The {k:F12} menu works inside it and it
has the same Edit menu as an unstructured document.
.table 17 43
Key | Effect
{k:Ins} | Keeps the changes: the page is put back into the document, and
 the editor ends.  ({k:Ins} is not Append section here.)
{k:Esc} | Throws the changes away and goes back to the text, with the page
 as it was.  Esc in a highlight cancels only the highlight.
All the other keys | The keys of editing work as in the text: typing,
 Delete, Backspace, Return, Tab, the attribute keys, Copy, Cut, Paste, Move,
 Split, Join, Append block, the block format editor, Marker, Search and
 Replace.
.end
.table 24 36
Page that is | Result
Not empty | Its blocks are put in the document as the title page.
Empty (one block with no text and no formats), or no blocks | The title
 page is removed: when there was one, it goes, and the document has none
 again.
Had a title page and is changed | Its blocks are replaced by the new ones.
.end
A document that had no title page opens the editor with an empty page to type
into.  The title page is not shown among the text and is not counted in the
numbers of the sections: the first section of the text is still 1.  The
document is marked as changed when the page is kept.  If the page that was
typed cannot be read back (a message box titled "Title Page" with the
reason), the old page is left as it was and the editor ends.

The page is part of the document when it is printed: the panel of Print,
Final has the question "Print Title Page?" and the preview panel has "Preview
Title Page?" ({n:print}).  Search, Replace and Count work on the title page
like on the text.

.h3 Windows [edit-b-window]
.ix Window
.ix Windows, two
.ix Edit, Window
.ix Zoom-up
.ix Zoom-down
.ix Switch (window)
Edit, Window shows two documents on the screen at the same time, each in a
window of its own.  A window has its own status line, expanded line and ruler,
and its own text.  The cursor is in one window; the commands work on the
document of that window.  The entries of Edit, Window are:
.table 14 44
Entry | Effect
Open | Splits the screen in two and opens a second window with a new unstructured document.  The cursor goes to the new window.  Retrieve a document in it, or write a new one.  The entry is available when one window is open.
Close | Closes the window of the cursor.  A document with unsaved changes prompts with "Save and close", "Discard changes and close" or "Cancel".  The other window fills the screen.
Adjust | Changes the size of the windows.  {k:Up} moves the line between the windows up one row and {k:Down} moves it down one row; {k:Enter} keeps the size and {k:Esc} puts it back.
Zoom-up | The window of the cursor fills the screen.  The other window stays open and is hidden.
Zoom-down | The two windows share the screen again.
Switch | Moves the cursor to the other window.  When a window fills the screen, the other one takes its place.
.end
.pic pictures/edit-b-window-menu.png 5 Edit, Window: the entries, with Open chosen and its meaning on the line below.
.pic pictures/edit-b-window-open.png 5 After Open the screen is shared by two windows, each with its own status line, ruler and document.
A window is at least five rows high.  Copy ({k:F3}) or Cut ({k:F4}) in one window
and Paste ({k:F5}) in the other carry text, blocks, columns or sections from
one document to the other: the clipboard goes with the cursor.  Quit
({k:Ctrl-Q}) puts a question about the unsaved changes of both documents.  Autosave saves
the document of the window of the cursor only.

.h3 Notes, Comments, Equations and the Library
The text of a note, comment or equation marker, and the text of an entry of
the library of the Tools menu, are edited in the same way as the title
page: a second editor opens on the text alone, the status line shows the
kind of text and the word EDIT (NOTE EDIT, COMMENT EDIT, EQUATION EDIT,
LIBRARY CONTENTS EDIT), and {k:Ins} keeps the text while {k:Esc} throws the
changes away.  All the editing keys of this chapter work in those editors,
with these exceptions: {k:Ins} and {k:Esc} are theirs (so a section cannot
be appended with {k:Ins} in them: use the {k:Ctrl-O} key for a block), the
document of a note is unstructured, and Quit and Save are better left
alone until the editor of the note has been closed.  While a highlight is on,
{k:Ins} and {k:Esc} belong to the highlight, not to the editor of the note.
The editor of an equation has a key of its own, {k:Ctrl-W}, which shows the
equation as it is typeset ({n:equations}).

.h2 The menus [edit-menus]
.ix Menu bar
.ix F12
.ix Menu bar keys
.ix MENU, label of the menu bar
.ix Pop-up lists
.ix Pop-up list keys
.ix Prompt line, single
.ix Enter accepts, Esc cancels
.ix Pop-up input
Strata has two kinds of menu on the edit screen: the menu bar that {k:F12}
opens, with the menus of the commands of the program, and the small
pop-up lists and prompts that the editing commands open themselves.

.h3 The F12 Menu Bar [edit-menubar]
.ix F12 menu bar
.ix Menu bar, keys
.ix Edit menu
.pic pictures/edit-a-menubar.png 5.5 The menu bar opened with F12: the label of the bar is MENU, the bar names the menus and the second line explains the one selected.
.pic pictures/edit-a-menubar-tools.png 5.5 The menu bar with Tools selected: the second line says what the selected entry holds.
{k:F12} puts a bar over the top two rows of the screen.  The first row names
the menus: File, Global, Edit, Print, Tools, Help and Quit.  The second row
explains the entry that is selected.  At the right end of the first row is
the label of the bar: MENU, and, as submenus open, MENU-EDIT, MENU-TOOLS and
so on.  When the entries of a menu do not all fit in the first row, as with
the Print menu on a screen of 80 columns, the rest continue on a second row
and the explanation moves one row down.  The text under the bar is not moved.
.table 20 40
Key | What it does
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | The previous or the next entry; round at the ends.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first or the last entry.
{k:Return}, {k:Ins}, {k:Down} | Opens the submenu, or runs the entry.
A letter | The entry that starts with it: selected and opened at once.
{k:Esc} | Back one level; at the top, closes the bar.
{k:F12} | Closes the bar from any level.
.end
{k:Down} does not move down: the submenus are rows of words like the bar,
and Down opens or runs the entry.  The entries are:
.table 7 53
Menu | Entries
File | Save, Retrieve, New, Delete, Import, Export ({n:file}).
Global | Format, Page, Typeface, Marker, Options, Named (Edit, Delete).
Edit | Title, Structured or Unstructured, Section or Document, Window
 (Open, Close, Adjust, Zoom-up, Zoom-down, Switch: {n:edit-b-window}), and the
 four groups of commands Block, Parts, Layout and Insert ({n:edit-menu-routes}).
Print | Draft, Final, Preview (Document, Block, Section), and the submenu
 Errors with Next, Previous, Remove and Remove All for the Print Errors
 ({n:print}).
Tools | Macros, Library (Edit, Delete, Save, Retrieve, Copy, Cut, Paste),
 Spell, Thesaurus, Autosort, Count, Undo, Redo, the group Find (Search,
 Replace, Again, Go To) and Math ({n:edit-menu-routes}).
Help | The help of the edit screen, as {k:F1} shows it.
Quit | Leave the Editor ({n:edit-b-quit}).
.end
The bar of the menus fits in 80 columns.  The row of the entries of a menu
that is wider than that, as the Print menu is, goes on a second row and the
hint line is one row lower; {k:Right} reaches every entry and the hint line
shows which one is chosen.  The letters of the entries are: Edit {k:T}, {k:S},
{k:U}, {k:D} (Title, Section or Structured, Unstructured, Document), {k:W}
(Window), {k:B}, {k:P}, {k:L}, {k:I} (Block, Parts, Layout, Insert); Tools
{k:M}, {k:L}, {k:S}, {k:T}, {k:A}, {k:C}, {k:U}, {k:R}, {k:F} (Macros,
Library, Spell, Thesaurus, Autosort, Count, Undo, Redo, Find); Autosort {k:S}
and {k:T} (Section, Table).  When two entries of a menu begin with the same
letter, the letter chooses the first of them and {k:Right} reaches the other:
Math has the letter of Macros, and Cut has the letter of Copy.

.h4 The commands of the function keys on the menu [edit-menu-routes]
.ix Menu entries of the function keys
.ix Function keys, entries on the menu
Every command that has a function key also has an entry in the {k:F12} menu, so
that a key that a terminal or a desktop keeps for itself still has a way.  An
entry runs the command of its key, with the same panel or the same message
that the key gives.  The entries are:
.table 16 14 28
Command | Key | Entry on the menu
Help | {k:F1} | {k:F12}, Help
Split | {k:F2} | {k:F12}, Edit, Parts, Split
Copy | {k:F3} | {k:F12}, Edit, Block, Copy
Cut | {k:F4} | {k:F12}, Edit, Block, Cut
Paste | {k:F5} | {k:F12}, Edit, Block, Paste
Format | {k:F6} | {k:F12}, Edit, Layout, Format
Move | {k:F7} | {k:F12}, Edit, Block, Move
Table | {k:F8} | {k:F12}, Edit, Layout, Table
Search | {k:F9} | {k:F12}, Tools, Find, Search
Math | {k:Ctrl-F2} | {k:F12}, Tools, Math
Lib Copy | {k:Ctrl-F3} | {k:F12}, Tools, Library, Copy
Lib Cut | {k:Ctrl-F4} | {k:F12}, Tools, Library, Cut
Lib Paste | {k:Ctrl-F5} | {k:F12}, Tools, Library, Paste
Case | {k:Ctrl-F6} | {k:F12}, Edit, Layout, Case
Prev Error | {k:Ctrl-F7} | {k:F12}, Print, Errors, Previous
Next Error | {k:Ctrl-F8} | {k:F12}, Print, Errors, Next
Search Again | {k:Ctrl-F9} | {k:F12}, Tools, Find, Again
Marker | {k:Ctrl-F12} | {k:F12}, Edit, Insert, Marker
Append | {k:Alt-F2} | {k:F12}, Edit, Parts, Append
Join | {k:Alt-F3} | {k:F12}, Edit, Parts, Join
Delete | {k:Alt-F4} | {k:F12}, Edit, Block, Delete
Overtype | {k:Alt-F5} | {k:F12}, Edit, Insert, Overtype
Set Attribute | {k:Alt-F6} | {k:F12}, Edit, Layout, Attribute
Go To | {k:Alt-F7} | {k:F12}, Tools, Find, Go To
Border | {k:Alt-F8} | {k:F12}, Edit, Layout, Border
Replace | {k:Alt-F9} | {k:F12}, Tools, Find, Replace
.end
The commands that already had an entry keep it: Learn ({k:Ctrl-F1}) is
{k:F12}, Tools, Macros, Learn, Undo ({k:Ctrl-F10}) and Redo ({k:Ctrl-F11}) are
in Tools, and Next Error ({k:Ctrl-F8}) and Previous Error ({k:Ctrl-F7}) are in
Print, Errors.  The keys that contract and expand are {k:Alt--}, {k:Alt-+} and
{k:Alt-*}; the other commands, and where to reach them, are:
.table 23 8 27
Command | Menu | Keys
Count | Tools, Count | none
Options | Global, Options | none
Outlines | None | {k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9}, {k:Alt-0}
Quit | Quit | {k:Ctrl-Q}
Section/Document | Edit, Section or Document | {k:Ctrl-^}
Sort | Tools, Autosort | none
Structured/Unstructured | Edit, one of them | none
Title | Edit, Title | none
.end
Some commands have other keys as well: Move works on a section level with
{k:Alt-[} and {k:Alt-]}, Overtype is also {k:Alt-O}, Paste is also {k:Ctrl-F5}
for the library, and Split is also {k:Ctrl-V} for a block and {k:Ctrl-Y} for a
section.
.h4 The Edit menu
The entries depend on the document:
.table 26 34
The document is | The Edit menu offers, before the groups
Unstructured | Title, Structured, Window
Structured, and it can be made unstructured (it is one section with nothing
 below it) | Title, Unstructured, Section, Window
Structured, and it cannot be made unstructured | Title, Section, Window
Being edited in Section edit, and it can be made unstructured | Title,
 Unstructured, Document, Window
Being edited in Section edit, and it cannot | Title, Document, Window
.end
After these entries, the four groups Block, Parts, Layout and Insert follow in
every document ({n:edit-menu-routes}).
Each entry is described in {x:edit-modes}: Title in {x:edit-b-title},
Structured and Unstructured in {x:edit-b-structured}, Section and Document in
{x:edit-b-section}.

.h3 The Alt Commands Menu [edit-altmenu]
.ix Alt commands, menu
.ix Ctrl-F, the menu of the Alt commands
.ix Alt commands menu
.ix Commands list
{k:Ctrl-F} opens a list titled Commands with every command that stands on a
key such as {k:F3}, {k:Ctrl-F9} or {k:Alt-F8}, each with its key, and three more commands whose keys a terminal may not
pass ({k:Alt-[}, {k:Alt-]} and the soft hyphen).  It works in every terminal,
where many of the keys with {k:Alt} or {k:Ctrl} do not arrive.  Choose with
{k:Up}, {k:Down}, {k:Home}, {k:End}, {k:PgUp} and {k:PgDn}, and run with
{k:Return} or {k:Ins}; the first letter of an entry goes to the next entry that begins
with it; {k:Esc} closes the list.  The list scrolls: it has thirty-four
entries.
.table 25 36
Entry | The key it stands for
{c:Help (F1)} | {k:F1}
{c:Split (F2)} | {k:F2}
{c:Copy (F3)} | {k:F3}
{c:Cut (F4)} | {k:F4}
{c:Paste (F5)} | {k:F5}
{c:Format (F6)} | {k:F6}
{c:Move (F7)} | {k:F7}
{c:Table (F8)} | {k:F8}
{c:Search (F9)} | {k:F9}
{c:Menu (F12)} | {k:F12}
{c:Learn (Ctrl-F1)} | {k:Ctrl-F1}
{c:Math (Ctrl-F2)} | {k:Ctrl-F2}
{c:Lib Copy (Ctrl-F3)} | {k:Ctrl-F3}
{c:Lib Cut (Ctrl-F4)} | {k:Ctrl-F4}
{c:Lib Paste (Ctrl-F5)} | {k:Ctrl-F5}
{c:Case (Ctrl-F6)} | {k:Ctrl-F6}
{c:Prev Error (Ctrl-F7)} | {k:Ctrl-F7}
{c:Next Error (Ctrl-F8)} | {k:Ctrl-F8}
{c:Search Again (Ctrl-F9)} | {k:Ctrl-F9}
{c:Undo (Ctrl-F10)} | {k:Ctrl-F10}
{c:Redo (Ctrl-F11)} | {k:Ctrl-F11}
{c:Marker (Ctrl-F12)} | {k:Ctrl-F12}
{c:Append (Alt-F2)} | {k:Alt-F2}
{c:Join (Alt-F3)} | {k:Alt-F3}
{c:Delete (Alt-F4)} | {k:Alt-F4}
{c:Overtype (Alt-F5)} | {k:Alt-F5}
{c:Set Attribute (Alt-F6)} | {k:Alt-F6}
{c:Go To (Alt-F7)} | {k:Alt-F7}
{c:Border (Alt-F8)} | {k:Alt-F8}
{c:Replace (Alt-F9)} | {k:Alt-F9}
{c:Marker (Alt-F12)} | {k:Alt-F12}
{c:Promote section (Alt-[)} | {k:Alt-[}
{c:Demote section (Alt-])} | {k:Alt-]}
{c:Soft hyphen (Alt-_)} | {k:Alt-_}
.end

.h3 The pop-up lists [edit-popups]
.ix Pop-up list
.ix Pop-up menu
.ix Selection list
.ix First letter selects
The commands Append, Copy, Cut, Move, Delete, Join, Split, Go To, Set
Attributes, the {k:F8} Table list and the Alt commands menu open a small box
with a title and a list of choices.  The keys are the same for all of them.
.table 15 45
Key | What it does
{k:Up}, {k:Down} | The previous or the next choice; no wrap at the ends.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first or the last choice.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | A page up or down, when the list is longer than the box.
{k:Return}, {k:Ins} | Takes the selected choice.
{k:F1} | Help for the list.
A letter | The next choice that starts with the letter; with 12 choices or
 fewer it is taken at once.
{k:Esc} | Closes the list; nothing is done.
.end
The first letter is a quick way to a command: {k:Alt-F2} then {k:C} appends a
column, {k:Alt-F2} then {k:P} a peer section, {k:F3} then {k:S} copies a section.
When two choices start with the same letter, the key takes the first after
the one that is selected, and the second press the other.  The box of
Append and Split opens with Block selected, the box of Set Attributes with
Bold selected, the others with the first choice.

.h3 The prompts [edit-prompts]
.ix Prompt
.ix Input prompt
.ix Enter accepts, Esc cancels
The commands that need a word or a number (Go To, Section number) open a box
with a title, a line of text, a field of dots and the words "Enter accepts,
Esc cancels".  The field shows the value that the command offers, drawn as a
selected entry; Go To offers none, so its field is empty.  The
panels with several fields, such as Search and the panels of the markers, are
forms: {k:Down}, {k:Tab} and {k:Enter} go to the next field, {k:Up} to the
previous one, {k:Ins} accepts and {k:Esc} cancels ({n:view-form-keys}).
.table 15 45
Key | What it does
A character | Replaces the whole field with it, the first time; then
 inserts.
{k:Backspace} | Empties the field the first time; then deletes backward.
{k:Del} | Deletes the character at the cursor.
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | Move in the field; the value is kept.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The start and the end of the field.
{k:Enter} | Accepts the value.
{k:Esc} | Cancels the command.
.end
Because the first character replaces the old value, a value that is to be
changed a little is edited by pressing a movement key first, which keeps it.

